  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that the rocket's height is $256$ feet, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(t) & =256 \\\\ -16{t}^{2}+128t & =256 \\\\ -16{t}^{2}+128t-256 & =0 \\\\ -16({t}^{2}-8t+16) & =0 \\\\ -16(t-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t-4 & =0 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing when $t=4$ seconds is equal to ${h}^{\u2032}(4).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $h(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-16{t}^{2}+128t) \\\\  & =-32t+128,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=4$ gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{\u2032}(4)=-32(4)+128=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing when $t=4$ seconds is $0$ feet per second.",
